<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shivappa Nayaka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1660">1660</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shivappa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> was the greatest ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi">Keladi</a> Nayaka kingdom in coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, reigning from 1645 to 1660. He introduced a celebrated land revenue system known as the shist, based on a survey of the land. He extended the kingdom along the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanara">Kanara</a>, driving the Portuguese from several ports, and built forts and irrigation works.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shivappa-nayaka.jpg" alt="Shivappa Nayaka" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: self · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shivappanayaka.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Shivappa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a>, popularly called Keladi Shivappa Nayaka, came to the throne in 1645. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi-nayakas">Keladi Nayakas</a> had succeeded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> in the coastal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malnad">Malnad</a> hill districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> in the late 16th century. When Shriranga Raya III, the last ruler of the diminished Vijayanagara Empire, who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vellore">Vellore</a>, was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Bijapur Sultanate</a>, he sought refuge with Shivappa, who received him and gave him charge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a> and Sakrepatna.</p><p>By 1653 the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalore">Mangalore</a>, Kundapura and Honnavar were under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi">Keladi</a> control. After conquering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanara">Kanara</a> coast Shivappa marched into the Kasargod region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> and set up a victory pillar at Neeleeswaram, and he built the forts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragiri">Chandragiri</a>, Bekal, Adka, Arikady and Mangalore. In 1654 he crossed the Tungabhadra and took territory in the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharwad">Dharwad</a> district from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>. In 1659 he laid siege to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a>, but an epidemic in his army forced him to withdraw.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His revenue settlement, the Sist, earned him the name Sistina Shivappa Nayaka and has been compared favourably with the revenue schemes of the Mogul emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>. Farmland was divided into five types by soil and irrigation, and a unit of sowing capacity called the khanduga was used to tax irrigable land. The rate varied from village to village and came to a third of the yield; his attention to farming expanded the agrarian economy.</p><p>The Ujjaini peeta served as rajaguru to the dynasty. Shivappa performed padapooja of Jangams and built many Veerashaiva maths, held Vedic sacrifices and patronised the Advaita order of Sringeri. He gave land to Christians to cultivate and encouraged merchant communities such as the Komatis and Konkanis to settle and trade in his kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Under Shivappa the kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi-nayakas">Nayakas of Keladi</a> reached its height, taking in the coastal, hill and some interior districts, the Bayaluseeme, of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. A story of his rule tells of Ganesh Mallya, a poor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> who set up an illegal ninth toll gate outside the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi">Keladi</a> and kept it for eighteen months; summoned before the king, he confessed, and Shivappa, impressed by his honesty and business sense, took him into his service. His son and successor Soma Shekhar was murdered, and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a> was placed on the throne under his mother's regency.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shivappa Nayaka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Shivappa Nayaka</b>, the greatest ruler, introduced a celebrated land revenue system, the shist, and expanded the kingdom to the coast of Kanara, driving the Portuguese from several ports.</blockquote><a href="/state/keladi-nayaka-kingdom">Keladi Nayaka kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
